**Reused evidence**
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0001"],"lenses":["5B.2"]}
- Kept as formal-authority context only: the president’s power to appoint ministers. It does not by itself prove competent appointments.

**New evidence**
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"New Support Will Help Strengthen Indonesia’s Tax System, Improve Development Spending","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-06-24","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/06/24/new-support-will-help-strengthen-indonesia-tax-system-improve-development-spending","claim":"The World Bank approved a $750 million loan to help Indonesia increase tax revenue, improve tax system equity, and strengthen institutions for more efficient planning and spending; the release says Indonesia’s tax revenues and spending on public investment, health, and social protection lag peers.","locator":"opening paragraphs and first policy block after the dateline","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official World Bank release with direct policy description and named ministerial attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly concerns Indonesia’s 2022 fiscal-reform agenda and implementation window.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Jokowi’s government through Minister of Finance Sri Mulyani Indrawati, who is quoted describing the reform agenda.","contrary_evidence":["This is a government-program description, not proof that Jokowi personally designed every reform detail.","The release describes intended reforms and financing, not full realized outcomes in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.4","5B.6","5B.7","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President Jokowi: Do Not Complicate Investment","publisher":"Sekretariat Kabinet Republik Indonesia","publication_date":"2022-11-30","url":"https://setkab.go.id/en/president-jokowi-do-not-complicate-investment/","claim":"At the 2022 National Coordination Meeting on Investment, Jokowi ordered all levels of government not to complicate investment and to speed up the OSS licensing system, saying investment is a key growth driver.","locator":"headline and opening speech paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential speech transcript published by the Cabinet Secretariat.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct ruler action in November 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Jokowi as the speaking president and policy director.","contrary_evidence":["Rhetoric does not prove implementation.","Later reporting still found legal and administrative conflict around investment and labor rules."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.4","5B.6","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Pemerintah Alihkan Subsidi BBM Agar Lebih Tepat Sasaran","publisher":"Sekretariat Kabinet Republik Indonesia","publication_date":"2022-09-03","url":"https://setkab.go.id/pemerintah-alihkan-subsidi-bbm-agar-lebih-tepat-sasaran/","claim":"Jokowi announced the reallocation of fuel subsidy funds because more than 70% of the subsidy benefited people who could afford it; the subsidy and compensation budget rose from Rp152.5 trillion to Rp502.4 trillion, with targeted BLT and wage support added.","locator":"body paragraphs after the dateline and the quoted presidential remarks","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential announcement with concrete fiscal figures and policy rationale.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Squarely in September 2022 during the fuel-price shock response.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Jokowi and his cabinet as the policy makers announcing the transfer.","contrary_evidence":["Higher fuel prices imposed real costs on households.","The policy was also a response to budget pressure, not pure distributive generosity."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.7","5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Untuk Melindungi Masyarakat Miskin dan Rentan Miskin, Pemerintah Alokasikan Dana BLT BBM Rp24,17 Triliun","publisher":"Kementerian Keuangan Republik Indonesia","publication_date":"2022-09-19","url":"https://www.kemenkeu.go.id/informasi-publik/publikasi/berita-utama/BLT-BBM","claim":"The finance ministry said the fuel-subsidy transfer package totaled Rp24.17 trillion and included BLT BBM, wage subsidies, and local-government transport support to shield poor and vulnerable households from price shocks.","locator":"opening paragraphs and quoted ministerial remarks","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official finance-ministry release with detailed budget and beneficiary figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly describes 2022 crisis response after the fuel-price adjustment.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Jokowi’s government via the finance ministry and presidential policy response.","contrary_evidence":["Effectiveness is asserted by the ministry, not independently measured here.","The package was reactive to a price increase the government itself imposed."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Monetary Policy Review - December 2022","publisher":"Bank Indonesia","publication_date":"2022-12-27","url":"https://www.bi.go.id/en/publikasi/laporan/Pages/Tinjauan-Kebijakan-Moneter-Desember-2022.aspx","claim":"Bank Indonesia said CPI inflation ended 2022 at 5.51 percent after subsidized fuel prices were adjusted in September 2022, and that close Government-BI coordination produced a front-loaded, pre-emptive response with core inflation at 3.36 percent.","locator":"opening summary paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Central-bank report with direct inflation and policy-response data.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"End-of-2022 macro and policy assessment for the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Only partially attributable to Jokowi because BI is independent, but the report explicitly credits government-BI coordination after the fuel-price move.","contrary_evidence":["Inflation still exceeded the target band.","The central bank, not the president alone, drove monetary stabilization."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Indonesia GDP Growth Rate 5.31 Percent (2022)","publisher":"BPS-Statistics Indonesia","publication_date":"2023-02-06","url":"https://www.bps.go.id/id/pressrelease/2023/02/06/1997/ekonomi-indonesia-tahun-2022-tumbuh-5-31-persen.html","claim":"BPS said Indonesia’s economy grew 5.31 percent in 2022, GDP per capita reached Rp71.0 million or US$4,783.9, and exports were the strongest growth component.","locator":"abstract bullets","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official national statistics release with year-end macro figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly measures 2022 macro performance.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Jokowi’s government, but also shaped by reopening and commodity prices.","contrary_evidence":["The result reflects strong external commodity conditions and post-COVID reopening as well as domestic policy.","Growth alone does not prove broad-based prosperity."],"lenses":["5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"August 2022: The unemployment rate was 5.86 percent and The average labour wage was 3.07 milion rupiahs per month","publisher":"BPS-Statistics Indonesia","publication_date":"2022-11-07","url":"https://www.bps.go.id/en/pressrelease/2022/11/07/1916/agustus-2022--tingkat-pengangguran-terbuka--tpt--/","claim":"BPS reported that August 2022 unemployment was 5.86 percent, the number of employed people increased by 4.25 million from August 2021, and the formal sector share rose to 40.69 percent.","locator":"abstract bullets","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official labor-market release with year-over-year figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 labor-market outcome.","ruler_attribution":"Macro outcome partly attributable to Jokowi’s government, but not exclusively; labor markets also reflected post-pandemic recovery.","contrary_evidence":["The release does not isolate policy causation.","Underemployment and wage levels still leave room for concern about job quality."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"In September 2022, the poor population percentage was up to 9.57 percent.","publisher":"BPS-Statistics Indonesia","publication_date":"2023-01-16","url":"https://www.bps.go.id/id/pressrelease/2023/01/16/2015/persentase-penduduk-miskin-september-2022-naik-menjadi-9-57-persen","claim":"BPS reported poverty at 9.57 percent in September 2022, up 0.03 percentage points from March 2022 but down from September 2021, with rural poverty at 12.36 percent and urban poverty at 7.53 percent.","locator":"abstract bullets","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official national-statistics release with household-poverty and regional breakdowns.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly measures 2022 household welfare.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Jokowi’s policy mix, but the report also reflects inherited poverty structure and macro shocks.","contrary_evidence":["Poverty improved versus September 2021, so the year does not show simple deterioration.","The data do not by themselves prove government causation."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Gini Ratio in September 2022 was 0.381","publisher":"BPS-Statistics Indonesia","publication_date":"2023-01-16","url":"https://www.bps.go.id/en/pressrelease/2023/01/16/2034/gini-ratio-september-2022-tercatat-sebesar-0-381.html/","claim":"BPS reported a September 2022 Gini ratio of 0.381, unchanged from September 2021 and down from March 2022; the bottom 40 percent accounted for 18.24 percent of expenditure.","locator":"abstract bullets","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official inequality release with national and regional breakdowns.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 distribution outcome.","ruler_attribution":"Only partly attributable to Jokowi; inequality outcomes also reflect long-run structure and external conditions.","contrary_evidence":["The level still indicates meaningful inequality.","Stable inequality is not the same as broad-based gain."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Indonesia Economic Prospects (IEP), June 2022: Financial Deepening for Stronger Growth and Sustainable Recovery","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-06","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/country/indonesia/publication/indonesia-economic-prospects-iep-june-2022-financial-deepening-for-stronger-growth-and-sustainable-recovery","claim":"The World Bank said Indonesia needed deeper financial markets, noted the 2022 budget reduced extraordinary COVID support and refocused on health and the war in Ukraine, and recommended tax reforms, a better business environment, greener downstream industries, and financial deepening to sustain growth.","locator":"key findings section with ten numbered findings","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official World Bank country report with explicit policy recommendations and macro context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Mid-2022 assessment of the same year’s policy stance and outlook.","ruler_attribution":"Government-level attribution under Jokowi, with specific spending and reform choices in his administration.","contrary_evidence":["Much of the text is analytical and prescriptive rather than a record of implementation.","Some macro improvement was due to commodity and reopening effects."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.4","5B.6","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"New Financing Will Support Indonesia to Improve and Protect Human Capital","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-07-05","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/07/05/new-financing-will-support-indonesia-to-improve-and-protect-human-capital","claim":"The World Bank approved a $350 million loan to improve human capital through income protection, expanded health access, healthier behavior, and more effective government spending; it said Indonesia’s Human Capital Index improved from 0.50 to 0.54 between 2010 and 2020 but remained below peers.","locator":"opening paragraphs and key bullet points","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official World Bank financing announcement with explicit human-capital metrics.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant to 2022 productivity and welfare policy.","ruler_attribution":"Government-level attribution under Jokowi’s administration; the loan reflects state policy rather than a private-sector outcome.","contrary_evidence":["The improvement in HCI predates 2022.","The report says Indonesia still lagged peer countries."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Corruption Perceptions Index - Explore Indonesia","publisher":"Transparency International","publication_date":"2023-01-31","url":"https://www.transparency.org/en/cpi/2022/index/idn","claim":"Transparency International’s 2022 CPI gave Indonesia a score of 34/100 and rank 110/180, down 4 points from 2021, and said anti-corruption efforts had stagnated.","locator":"country score panel and Asia-Pacific commentary","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary CPI publication from the source organization that owns the index.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Measures the 2022 corruption environment and policy trajectory.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Jokowi’s administration, but CPI is a perception index and not a case-level finding.","contrary_evidence":["Perception scores are not direct proof of specific ruler orders.","The CPI blends expert and business perceptions across institutions, not just presidential conduct."],"lenses":["5B.5","5B.7","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Indonesia issues emergency regulation to replace jobs law","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-12-29","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/indonesia-issues-emergency-regulation-to-replace-jobs-law-2971257","claim":"Reuters reported that Jokowi signed an emergency regulation to replace a court-flawed jobs law, arguing global economic uncertainty justified the move; critics said it bypassed proper parliamentary debate, while the government said the law was needed for legal certainty and investment.","locator":"article body paragraphs 1-8","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting with named officials and direct quotes, republished on a commercial site.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late-2022 ruler action directly affecting investment rules and labor regulation.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Jokowi as signatory and public defender of the decree.","contrary_evidence":["Government said global uncertainty and the court deadline required a legal fix.","Supporters said it reduced investment uncertainty; opponents said it undermined consultation and labor and environmental protections."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Indonesia to review minimum wage rules after protests over fuel price hike","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-09-12","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/indonesia-to-review-minimum-wage-rules-after-protests-over-fuel-price-hike-2890672","claim":"After nationwide protests over the September 2022 petrol price hike, the president’s office said Indonesia would review minimum wage and other labor rules; Reuters also reported that Jokowi had raised subsidised fuel prices by 30% to rein in the subsidy budget.","locator":"article body paragraphs 1-6","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting with direct references to the presidential office and the fuel-price decision.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct September 2022 policy response to an economic shock.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Jokowi’s fuel-price decision and the presidential office’s subsequent review order.","contrary_evidence":["The policy was framed as fiscal necessity rather than distributive generosity.","The article shows both popular backlash and administrative response, not clean welfare success."],"lenses":["5B.7","5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Jokowi orders seventh Cabinet reshuffle","publisher":"The Jakarta Post","publication_date":"2022-06-15","url":"https://www.thejakartapost.com/paper/2022/06/15/jokowi-orders-seventh-cabinet-reshuffle","claim":"The Jakarta Post reported that analysts saw Jokowi’s June 2022 reshuffle as more political than technocratic, aimed at keeping coalition partners happy and filling the cabinet with loyalists; Jokowi said the new trade minister would oversee domestic goods supply and the agrarian minister land acquisition for the new capital.","locator":"middle and later paragraphs on cabinet motives and appointments","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Local-paper analysis with named analysts, direct quotes, and contemporaneous appointment details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 ruler period.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Jokowi’s cabinet choices.","contrary_evidence":["The presidency presented the reshuffle as a normal appointment decree and a way to refresh the cabinet.","The article reflects analyst interpretation, not a formal finding of improper motive."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.5"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Tax Revenue Reaches 110.06 Percent","publisher":"Sekretariat Kabinet Republik Indonesia","publication_date":"2022-12-20","url":"https://setkab.go.id/en/tax-revenue-reaches-110-06-percent/","claim":"Tax income had reached 110.06 percent of target by 14 December 2022 and grew 41.93 percent year over year; Sri Mulyani said this reflected good economic recovery, rising commodities, and reform of the tax law.","locator":"opening paragraphs and ministerial quote","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official presidential-secretariat republication of finance-ministry data and quoted explanation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"End-of-2022 fiscal-performance evidence for the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Jokowi’s administration through the finance ministry and tax policy apparatus.","contrary_evidence":["Commodity prices materially boosted receipts, so this is not purely a structural-policy effect.","The article reports achievement against target, not distributional quality."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I reviewed roughly 30 documents across World Bank/IMF reports, Bank Indonesia monthly reports, BPS macro releases, Kemenkeu and Setkab announcements, Transparency International CPI material, Reuters/Investing reporting, and Jakarta Post analysis.
- I kept the strongest non-duplicative items and removed near-duplicates such as multiple republications of the same Reuters story, overlapping subsidy-press releases that said the same thing in different wording, and general background pages that did not add a new 2022 ruler-specific claim.
- I rejected out-of-period or weak-fit material such as older background pages, 2023 follow-ups that only repeated a 2022 finding, and broad institutional pages without a ruler-period claim.
- I considered the IMF 2022 Article IV page, but the XML endpoint was blocked, so I relied on the accessible abstract plus stronger World Bank, BI, and BPS materials instead.
- I also checked trade-surplus and palm-oil-ban reporting, but those were either outcome-only or redundant with the subsidy-response evidence, so I did not retain them.
- For appointment competence, the evidence is mixed: the cabinet reshuffle coverage suggests political bargaining, while the formal appointment authority is clear from the reused constitutional source.

**Remaining gaps**
- Direct evidence on Jokowi’s personal economic-appointment judgment in 2022 is still thin. The cabinet reshuffle evidence is mostly indirect and mixed.
- More household-level distribution evidence would help, especially regional, gender, and bottom-quintile outcomes beyond the national poverty and Gini releases.
- Case-level corruption or favoritism evidence tied directly to presidential authority would strengthen 5B.5 beyond CPI and analyst commentary.
- More direct evidence on implementation quality for the jobs-law/emergency-regulation route would help separate growth-oriented reform from political expediency.
